# Feature Delivery Agent (FDA-001)

**Purpose:** Define a reusable, repository-level **architecture-aware feature delivery orchestrator** for the app-tour pnpm workspace monorepo. The agent discovers scope, classifies architecture, detects future consumers, implements a complete feature on a **locked branch**, verifies with evidence, and commits/pushes only when gates are green.

**Principle:** The agent is an orchestrator, not an autonomous architect. Static scripts may emit **signals**; **architecture decisions** require a read-only **architecture-reviewer** subagent (or equivalent human gate). The agent never silently changes branch, worktree, scope, architecture boundary, or product contract.

**Canonical Cursor paths (Phase B+):** `.cursor/rules/`, `.cursor/skills/`, `.cursor/agents/`, `.cursor/hooks.json`. This charter does not modify `.agents/skills/`.

---

## 1. Scope

### 1.1 In scope

| Concern | Agent responsibility |
| ------- | -------------------- |
| Discovery | Read feature request, existing standards, and code inventory |
| Architecture classification | Apply [architecture-classifier](feature-delivery/architecture-classifier.mdoc) |
| Consumer scan | Identify current and plausible future consumers before implementation |
| Planning | Produce a reviewable plan with checkpoints and verification matrix |
| User review gate | Pause for explicit approval before non-trivial implementation |
| Vertical slice | Deliver one end-to-end path before breadth |
| Mid-flight architecture review | Re-run classifier + architecture-reviewer at checkpoints |
| Verification | Run tiered tests/guards per [tiered-testing](tiered-testing.md) |
| Evidence report | Emit ledger-backed final report per [evidence-ledger-schema](feature-delivery/evidence-ledger-schema.mdoc) |
| Commit / push | Same locked branch only; scope-guarded staging |

### 1.2 Out of scope (this charter)

- Implementing unified notification platform (see [notification-case-study](feature-delivery/notification-case-study.mdoc))
- NX task graph orchestration (repo uses **pnpm workspace**, not NX)
- Overriding Cursor Cloud branch behavior — if actual branch ≠ locked branch, **hard-stop** (see §3)
- Automatic revert of user changes — use checkpoints or forward-fixes only
- Staging/production deploy, secret injection, migration rewrite

---

## 2. Session contract

### 2.1 Branch lock

At session start the agent records:

| Field | Semantics |
| ----- | --------- |
| `lockedBranch` | Branch name where the session started — **immutable for the session** |
| `initialHead` | `git rev-parse HEAD` at session start — **evidence baseline only** |
| `currentHead` | Updated after each authorized commit — **may change** |
| `scopePaths` | Glob allowlist derived **per feature session** at CP0 from the approved plan — not a repo constant |

**Rules:**

1. The agent **stays on `lockedBranch`** for the entire session.
2. If `git branch --show-current` ≠ `lockedBranch` at any checkpoint → **hard-stop** ([stop-conditions](feature-delivery/stop-conditions.mdoc) §1).
3. `initialHead` is evidence-only; authorized commits on `lockedBranch` may advance `currentHead` — **this is not a branch-lock violation**.
4. `scopePaths` are set at CP0 from the feature plan and may be amended only with user/architect approval.
5. Forbidden without explicit user unlock: `checkout`, `switch`, `merge`, `rebase`, `reset`, `clean`, `worktree`, force-push.

### 2.2 Working tree

- If the tree is **unexpectedly dirty** at session start (undeclared user WIP), record state and **hard-stop** until scope is acknowledged or user directs continuation.
- The agent **never automatically reverts** user changes. Wrong direction → checkpoint failure → pivot memo → forward-fix or user-directed revert.

### 2.3 Doc-first covenant

Changes under `packages/platform-core`, `packages/workspace-sdk`, or `apps/api` require a matching Markdoc update under `docs/` before merge — see [`.cursorrules`](../../.cursorrules) and [app-tour-architecture skill](../../.cursor/skills/app-tour-architecture/SKILL.md). State `Updating documentation for this change` before writing protected code.

---

## 3. Lifecycle

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  start[SessionStart] --> bootstrap[Bootstrap: record branch + initialHead]
  bootstrap --> branchOk{branch == lockedBranch?}
  branchOk -->|no| stopBranch[HardStop: branch mismatch]
  branchOk -->|yes| discover[Discovery + consumer scan]
  discover --> classify[Architecture classification]
  classify --> archReview[Architecture-reviewer read-only]
  archReview --> blocked{blocked or unknown?}
  blocked -->|yes| stopArch[HardStop: architect decision]
  blocked -->|no| plan[Plan + user review gate]
  plan --> slice[Vertical slice implementation]
  slice --> cp1[CP1: mid-flight arch review]
  cp1 --> cp2[CP2: pre-DB / shared contract]
  cp2 --> cp3[CP3: pre-UI integration]
  cp3 --> verify[Verification + evidence ledger]
  verify --> cp4[CP4: pre-commit scope guard]
  cp4 --> commit[Commit on locked branch]
  commit --> cp5[CP5: pre-push matrix]
  cp5 --> push[Push origin lockedBranch]
  push --> report[Evidence report]
```

### 3.1 Phase map

| Phase | Activities | Gate |
| ----- | ---------- | ---- |
| **Discovery** | Read standards, phase docs, grep/graph consumers | Bootstrap complete |
| **Architecture classification** | Module vs platform vs workspace plugin | Classifier output |
| **Consumer scan** | codebase-memory graph + docs cross-ref | architecture-reviewer |
| **Plan** | Checkpoints, scope paths, test matrix | User review gate |
| **Vertical slice** | One happy path (API or UI) | CP1 |
| **Mid-flight architecture review** | Re-classify actual diff | CP1, CP2, CP3 |
| **Verification** | Fast-track default; heavy gates only with YES | Evidence ledger rows |
| **Evidence report** | Every claim → command/artifact | Ledger complete |
| **Commit** | Logical commits; scope allowlist only | CP4 |
| **Push** | `git push origin <lockedBranch>` when matrix green | CP5 |

---

## 4. Checkpoints

| ID | When | Mandatory actions |
| -- | ---- | ----------------- |
| **CP0** | Before first file edit | Bootstrap; classifier; consumer scan; architecture-reviewer; scope path allowlist |
| **CP1** | After first vertical slice | Re-run classifier on diff; confirm no platform leakage |
| **CP2** | Before Prisma migration, new `*-http-contracts`, workspace-sdk export | Doc-first; RLS review; dedupe/idempotency; architecture-reviewer for multi-consumer candidates |
| **CP3** | Before Portal/Web routes, BFF, Playwright | WRS/PCMS guards; i18n; no raw upstream JSON in UI |
| **CP4** | Before `git commit` | Scope guard; `git diff --check`; fast-track verification; ledger row per command |
| **CP5** | Before `git push` | Feature test matrix green; branch/remote parity |

**Pivot (wrong direction):** Checkpoint failure produces a **Pivot Memo** (what is wrong, target boundary, docs to update, forward-fix steps). No silent continuation. No automatic revert.

### 4.1 User control and autonomy

| Principle | Behavior |
| --------- | -------- |
| **End-to-end execution** | After CP0 plan approval, the agent may implement, verify, commit, and push a complete feature **without repeated manual prompts** for each micro-step |
| **Mandatory gates** | Architecture-reviewer, CP1–CP5, doc-first, and evidence ledger remain **non-optional** |
| **Decision stops** | Unresolved product intent, shared-contract shape, migration strategy, security/RLS ambiguity, or scope expansion → **hard-stop** for user/architect review ([stop-conditions](feature-delivery/stop-conditions.mdoc)) |
| **No auto-revert** | User WIP and prior commits are never reverted automatically — checkpoint pivot or forward-fix only |
| **Evidence required** | Final report claims require ledger rows — no evidence, no claim ([evidence-ledger-schema](feature-delivery/evidence-ledger-schema.mdoc) §7) |

**Supported feature classes (non-exhaustive):** ticketing, wallet, booking/tour, payment/debt, notifications, profile/auth, Portal/Web UI, API/database/RLS — classifier and reviewer apply uniformly.

---

## 5. Architecture-reviewer

Static scripts (future Phase C) emit **signals** only — e.g. keyword hits, import-boundary violations, path classification hints.

**Final architecture decisions** require a read-only **architecture-reviewer**:

- Confirms or rejects classifier output
- Reviews multi-consumer platform candidates (notifications, attachments, identity, etc.)
- References design-freeze docs (e.g. [SK2_NOTIFICATION_OUTBOX](../phase-saas-kernel/appendices/SK2_NOTIFICATION_OUTBOX.md))
- Returns `proceed` | `pivot` | `blocked`

Keyword match alone is **insufficient** for a final decision — see [architecture-classifier](feature-delivery/architecture-classifier.mdoc) §4.

**Regression fixture:** [notification-case-study](feature-delivery/notification-case-study.mdoc) — architecture-reviewer must flag module-specific inbox when ≥2 consumers exist.

---

## 6. Verification policy

Default to fast-track per [tiered-testing](tiered-testing.md) and [`.cursorrules`](../../.cursorrules):

```bash
pnpm run pre-commit:fast && pnpm run guard:import-boundary
pnpm run test:changed   # when behavior changed
```

Heavy gates (`test:full`, `ci:integrity`, `phase-N:gate`) require explicit user **YES**.

**test-changed coverage gaps:** If a **changed package in the current feature** is not mapped in [`scripts/test-changed.sh`](../../scripts/test-changed.sh), record a **feature-scoped blocker** for that session, run the explicit `<targeted-test-command>` (e.g. `pnpm --filter @app-tour/<package-name> test`), and ledger both rows. Gaps do **not** block unrelated features. See [evidence-ledger-schema](feature-delivery/evidence-ledger-schema.mdoc) §6.

Postgres/RLS specs: `DATABASE_URL` must use the `app_tour` role (NOSUPERUSER), not superuser — invalid RLS proof if run as superuser.

---

## 7. Evidence and final report

All verification commands append rows to the session ledger per [evidence-ledger-schema](feature-delivery/evidence-ledger-schema.mdoc).

Final report sections (minimum):

1. Branch, `initialHead`, `currentHead`, working tree
2. Classification and architecture-reviewer verdict
3. Files changed (scope allowlist)
4. Test/guard matrix with pass/fail/skip
5. Artifacts (screenshots, logs)
6. Remaining risks / accepted risks
7. Commit SHAs and push result

**Rule:** No claim without a ledger row (command + exit code + artifact).

